./md-review-records/2026-08-13-sanity-checker-instructions/claude-hunt-floor.md:3:I read `docs/agents/sanity-checker-instructions.md` in full (41 lines, no YAML frontmatter), plus its explicitly-referenced paths (`agent-seat-model.md`, `docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md`, the scorecard, `subtract-cell-prompt-lessons.md`, the md-review machinery, PR #51/52/53, and the choirmaster transcript) to check claims made against them. Findings below, in document order.
./md-review-records/2026-08-13-sanity-checker-instructions/claude-hunt-floor.md:5:1. **Line 3:** `"The sanity-checker reviewer, the md-review grid it may join, ..."` combined with line 7's `"Does the sanity-checker join the md-review grid as three stance attacks ... run on both Fable and gpt-5.6-sol at xhigh?"` — the name "sanity-checker" is used for two different things without ever being disambiguated: (a) the agent seat that owns this pile and reads this very file (per `agent-seat-model.md`'s seat table: `sanity-checker | review quality: the sanity-checker reviewer and the md-review grid`), and (b) a reviewer persona/prompt candidate that "may join the md-review grid" as three stance-attack cells, defined in `docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md` (which itself opens "You are the sanity-checker. You receive one or more MD files..."). A reader has to infer from context alone that "does the sanity-checker join the grid" means "does the reviewer-prompt become a grid cell," not "does this agent seat join something." The term "grid-seat" (used later at line 41) is the file's only attempt at disambiguation, and it is itself introduced without definition (see finding 6). I'm sure this is a real overload; I'm unsure whether it causes practical harm given the file is short enough to read in full before acting.
./md-review-records/2026-08-13-sanity-checker-instructions/claude-hunt-floor.md:7:2. **Line 9:** `"The already-walked operating rulings — scope, trigger, order, models, piecemeal delivery, triage ownership — are in the header of `docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md`."` — I read that header: it contains nine ruled bullets (Scope and trigger, Order, Drift-lint script, Candidate seat shape, Models, The validation experiment gates the seat, Piecemeal return, No refuter tier, Code in evidence is a mode), not six. This sentence's list omits at least "the drift-lint script" and "code in evidence is a mode" outright, and doesn't clearly map "candidate seat shape" or "the validation experiment gates the seat" onto any listed item. Presented with the definite article ("**the** already-walked operating rulings") and a specific enumerated list, a reader would reasonably take this as the complete set. Unsure whether the omission is deliberate (e.g., considered out of scope for a summary) or an oversight, but as written it undercounts what's actually in the header.
./md-review-records/2026-08-13-sanity-checker-instructions/claude-hunt-floor.md:9:3. **Line 20:** `"Findings 1 and 2 are gatekeeper territory and 4 is fleet territory — triage them here, then hand the survivors to those seats rather than implementing them yourself."` — four findings were just enumerated (lines 15–18), but this disposition sentence assigns a destination only to findings 1, 2, and 4. Finding 3 ("A writer-stamps-the-pin proposal...") is never assigned a territory. The instruction "triage them here, then hand the survivors to those seats" leaves no destination named for finding 3's survivors — it's unclear whether it silently stays owned by sanity-checker, was meant to go somewhere unstated, or was simply dropped when the sentence was written. Sure this gap exists; the executing agent has no way to resolve it from this file alone.
./md-review-records/2026-08-13-sanity-checker-instructions/claude-hunt-floor.md:15:6. **Line 37:** `"Stated at S6 of the 2026-08-10 walk: "asking to simplify is like asking to optimize without context." State the axis."` — "State the axis." is a two-word imperative sentence with no stated addressee or object. It isn't clear whether it's an instruction to the sanity-checker seat (state which axis you're using, right now, in this document?), an instruction meant for the reviewer prompt being designed (a directive that findings must cite the axis), or a stray editorial fragment. Unsure how to execute it as written.
./md-review-records/2026-08-13-sanity-checker-instructions/claude-hunt-floor.md:17:7. **Line 37:** `"...never trade a deterministic script for probabilistic agent behavior."` — taken literally, this is an absolute barring any move from scripted mechanism to model judgment. But the very pile this file governs is deciding whether to add a probabilistic LLM reviewer (the sanity-checker itself, running as agent "attacks" at `xhigh`) to a review process — i.e., choosing agent judgment where a deterministic drift-lint script does not suffice (the header at `sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md` explicitly separates "mechanical worry" into a to-be-built drift-lint script from "the judgment review," implying some steps are conceded to need probabilistic judgment). If "never trade a deterministic script for probabilistic agent behavior" is read broadly, it's in tension with adopting any new agent-judgment step at all; if read narrowly ("never downgrade an existing deterministic mechanism to a probabilistic one"), it doesn't obviously apply here since no existing script is being replaced. I'm unsure which reading is intended, but the ordinary counterexample — this project builds and green-lights new agent-judgment reviewers regularly — shows the literal, broad reading doesn't hold.
