./md-review-records/2026-08-13-sanity-checker-instructions/claude-restate-good.md:5:## Heading — `` `sanity-checker` — seat instructions ``
./md-review-records/2026-08-13-sanity-checker-instructions/claude-restate-good.md:7:1. This document is the set of instructions for a role (a "seat") whose identifier is the literal string `sanity-checker`; the backticks around the name suggest it is a name used verbatim elsewhere in the system (a directory, an agent type, or a launcher argument), and "seat instructions" means the standing brief handed to whichever agent session occupies that role, rather than a description of a person or a one-off task.
./md-review-records/2026-08-13-sanity-checker-instructions/claude-restate-good.md:12:2. "The sanity-checker reviewer, the md-review grid it may join, and the skill rules that govern how reviews are delivered." — Spelling out three concrete things that fall inside that pile: (a) the reviewer entity itself, i.e. the sanity-checker as a review agent or review prompt that examines material; (b) something called the "md-review grid", which the sanity-checker is a candidate to become part of but is not yet part of — "grid" I read as a matrix of review cells, i.e. combinations of review-stance × model-runtime, each cell being one review run; (c) the rules encoded in a "skill" (in this harness, a packaged instruction set invoked by name) that determine the manner in which review output is handed to its recipient — for instance in what form, in what order, and at what pace.
./md-review-records/2026-08-13-sanity-checker-instructions/claude-restate-good.md:17:1. "**Does the sanity-checker join the md-review grid as three stance attacks** — cut, mechanization, fresh-eyes — run on both Fable and gpt-5.6-sol at xhigh?" — The open question, phrased as a yes/no to be put to the user, is whether the sanity-checker should be added into the md-review grid in the specific shape of three separate review passes, each taking a different adversarial posture ("stance attack" = one review run conducted from one deliberately narrow critical angle): one whose posture is to find things to cut, one whose posture is to find things that could be made mechanical (automated/deterministic rather than left to an agent's judgment), and one whose posture is to read the material as though seeing it for the first time. Each of those three would be executed twice — once on the model named Fable and once on the model named gpt-5.6-sol — with the reasoning-effort setting "xhigh" in both cases, yielding six runs in total. I read "join the grid" as "become standing cells in the grid that run as part of every md-review", not "be run once as an experiment".
./md-review-records/2026-08-13-sanity-checker-instructions/claude-restate-good.md:20:4. "The already-walked operating rulings — scope, trigger, order, models, piecemeal delivery, triage ownership — are in the header of `docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md`." — A set of operational decisions has already been presented to the user item by item and settled with him ("walked" = taken through the walk-me-through process, one item at a time, with his ruling on each), and those settled decisions are recorded at the top of the named draft file. The six settled matters are: what the sanity-checker's remit covers (scope); what causes it to run (trigger); the sequence in which its passes or outputs occur (order); which model runtimes it uses (models); the rule that its output is handed over in installments rather than all at once (piecemeal delivery); and who is responsible for sorting the resulting findings into keep/discard/route buckets (triage ownership).
