./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:19:9. [Line 11](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:11): “Your report goes to the agent that requested the review… the requesting agent triages your findings and walks them with the user, and only the findings he accepts reach the document.” The workflow has no branch for a human requester, a harness without a requesting agent, an absent user, or disagreement between the user and requesting agent. “Only the findings he accepts” also makes the requesting agent appear authoritative after saying the findings are walked with the user. These reachable cases leave delivery and decision authority unresolved. Confidence: unsure because an unstated harness may guarantee this exact topology.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:21:10. [Lines 11, 15, and 90](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:11): “your findings are design changes — unlike a comprehension fix”; “It can mean making the MD file easier to read and understand”; and “A wording-level trim is not worth reporting.” These establish incompatible scopes. A comprehension or wording defect can materially prevent understanding without changing the underlying design, but the first and third statements exclude it while the second includes it. No boundary distinguishes an in-scope readability change from an excluded comprehension fix. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:23:11. [Lines 17 and 47](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:17): “natural language prompts or instructions to LLMs can be replaced with code (the highest-value form…)” conflicts with “The deepest simplifications remove the need, not the text.” Deleting an unnecessary mechanism and encoding it are ranked differently depending on whether “highest-value” or “deepest” governs. Because findings must later be ordered by depth, the reviewer cannot derive a consistent ranking from these claims. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:25:12. [Line 20](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:20): “attempts to solve NP complete problems, like detecting every way a computer can edit a file.” “Detecting every way a computer can edit a file” is not a specified finite decision problem shown to be in NP; under ordinary unrestricted interpretations it is open-ended or undecidable, not NP-complete. Misclassifying it invokes the wrong computational concept and can misdirect the review. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:27:13. [Line 20](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:20): “In the case of guarding a file from edits, simply backing up that file, then checking if it has been altered.” Backing up and later comparing a file does not guard it from edits; it can only detect some changes after they happen. The mechanism also leaves timing, restoration, permitted edits, replacement or rename, and changes made and reverted between checks unresolved. It therefore neither performs its literal purpose nor defines its reachable cases. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:29:14. [Line 22](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:22): “the unfortunate tendency of AIs … to rarely or never simplify, delete or cut.” This is a population-wide empirical assertion without a model, task distribution, or evidence boundary. An ordinary counterexample is an agent explicitly asked to minimize a patch or remove dead code, which may simplify routinely. The claim can bias the reviewer toward manufacturing subtraction findings. Confidence: unsure because it may be intended as rhetoric about this project’s observed sessions rather than a universal claim.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:31:15. [Lines 22 and 51](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:22): “adding a second or third check to check if the first or second check is working” is presented as complexity that “will not make the overall design actually more robust,” while the method later asks, “can code mechanically check the result even where a model produces it?” Independent validation, watchdogs, and diverse checks are ordinary cases where checking a checker does improve robustness. The example therefore conflicts with the later verification rung unless an unstated distinction separates useful verification from useless meta-checking. Confidence: unsure because “unlikely and unimportant theoretical cases” may be intended to exclude useful independent checks, but the example itself does not say that.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:33:16. [Line 22](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:22): “every change you propose must leave the system better — simpler or more autonomous, safer or more testable, and saner or more reliable.” The Boolean structure is ambiguous: it can require one improvement from each of three pairs, or merely one improvement from a loose six-item list. Under the first reading, a maintainability-only improvement expressly allowed at line 18 cannot qualify; under the second, a change may worsen two entire dimensions. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:35:17. [Line 28](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:28): “zero remembered human steps.” Taken as an absolute design target, this is broader than systems can always support. Human consent, privileged approval, physical recovery, or emergency judgment can be irreducible operational steps. Treating zero as universally preferable can push a reviewer to remove necessary controls. Confidence: unsure because “remembered” may mean undocumented or manually recalled steps rather than all human action.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:37:18. [Line 36](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:36): “They replace LLM prompts or English instructions with code so that the steps, states, or algorithm is both hundreds of times faster, deterministic, followed exactly, and can be tested and tuned exactly.” None of those results follows merely from replacing instructions with code. Code can be slower, concurrent, randomized, environment-dependent, incorrectly implemented, or difficult to test; “hundreds of times” and “exactly” have no measurement basis. The malformed “both” also purports to join four different guarantees. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:39:19. [Line 36](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:36): “Ten, a hundred, or even a thousand lines of Python is in actuality simpler to debug than invoking an agent with a short prompt.” This absolute comparison fails for ordinary counterexamples such as a trivial classification prompt versus a thousand-line stateful, concurrent Python program. Debuggability depends on implementation, dependencies, observability, and failure mode, not line count or artifact type alone. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:41:20. [Line 36](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:36): “Good code works or doesn’t.” Code commonly works for some inputs, fails silently for others, degrades under load, is flaky, or satisfies one requirement while violating another. The binary claim hides exactly the partial and situational failures a reliability review must consider. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:43:21. [Line 36](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:36): “100% predictable, but far longer code.” Ordinary code that depends on clocks, networks, operating systems, resource limits, concurrency, external services, or malformed inputs is not 100% predictable. The unqualified guarantee can make the reviewer treat code migration itself as proof of correctness. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:45:22. [Line 40](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:40): “ranking alternatives no known libraries or algorithms cover.” “Known” has no subject or search boundary: known to the reviewer, the project, available dependencies, or the world. Establishing that no library or algorithm exists is an open-ended search with no stopping criterion, so the reviewer cannot execute this classification reliably. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:47:23. [Lines 45, 54, and 92](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:45): “For every component, step, state, or dependency … report the earliest one that applies” conflicts with “A step that works reliably today, costs little, and fails loudly is not a finding” and “report only the candidates that survive.” “Delegate the residue” will apply whenever the first five questions do not, so the first instruction literally requires a report entry for every component, while the latter instructions require suppressing many of them. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:49:24. [Lines 45–51](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:45): “report the earliest one that applies” assumes the six questions are mutually exclusive, but they overlap. Moving control flow into a script can satisfy both “Encode” and “Externalize”; code can both produce and verify a result. Reporting only the earliest suppresses later applicable transformations without stating that they are discarded or combined, leaving reachable multi-rung cases undefined. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:51:25. [Lines 47–51](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:47): “Delete,” “Encode,” “Constrain,” “Externalize,” and “Verify.” These one-word taxonomy names are not self-documenting in isolation—“Encode,” for example, ordinarily means serialization—and are highly noisy search terms. This conflicts with the checkout instruction to use “explicit, clear and precise multi-part names” for names likely to be grepped. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:53:26. [Line 48](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:48): “stable, easily understood code (a script, a standard query, a function, or a configuration).” A “standard query” can mean SQL, a search query, or a standard natural-language prompt, while configuration need not be code. Because the phrase claims these examples are unambiguously code-side, its literal categories support incompatible implementations. Confidence: unsure because context suggests executable queries and configuration, but it never states that boundary.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:55:27. [Line 54](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:54): “the mechanism you propose is itself new complexity, and it must pay for itself by preventing a real failure or removing a recurring cost.” A deletion finding may propose no new mechanism at all. The two permitted payoffs also exclude earlier in-scope benefits such as readability, modularity, maintainability, safer architecture, or improved testability before a failure has occurred. This makes the earning rule contradict the review’s stated scope. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:57:28. [Line 54](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:54): “A step that works reliably today, costs little, and fails loudly is not a finding.” A reliable, cheap, loud step may still compute an output with no consumer, duplicate a normative rule, or enforce an obsolete distinction—all classes the next section explicitly tells the reviewer to hunt. The absolute exclusion can therefore suppress the file’s own validated cut classes. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:59:29. [Line 58](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:58): “list every place the design relies on an LLM following English instructions where a script could do the job.” This requires listing even candidates that fail the earning rule or the self-refutation step, directly conflicting with “report only the candidates that survive.” “Could do the job” also has no materiality threshold, so almost any bounded model behavior can enter the list. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:61:30. [Line 59](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:59): “A better way” is a mandatory report-section name but is generic and search-noisy. It does not identify that the section concerns whole-design alternatives and missing assumptions, so a future maintainer cannot locate it reliably by its purpose. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:63:31. [Line 59](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:59): “are we missing something important, an unknown unknown?” By definition, an unknown unknown is not identifiable from the reviewer’s known evidence. The question provides no search space, evidence threshold, or stopping condition, so literal completion can continue indefinitely and a negative answer cannot be justified. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:65:32. [Line 66](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:66): “the same rule stated authoritatively in two places, guaranteed to drift apart.” Duplication does not guarantee drift. Ordinary counterexamples include generated copies, synchronized schemas, checked mirrors, and duplicated declarations protected by equivalence tests. The claim can cause a reviewer to condemn deliberately redundant but mechanically synchronized normative homes. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:67:33. [Line 67](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:67): “Carrier-vs-invariant collapse.” The coined name is not self-documenting: “carrier,” “invariant,” “versus,” and “collapse” do not reveal which thing is removed or derived. The following gloss makes the particular example understandable, but the name alone remains opaque when used in reports or search results. Confidence: unsure because the exact hyphenated phrase is searchable and receives an immediate gloss.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:69:34. [Line 67](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:67): “move the carrier, never drop the fact.” “Never” is broader than can hold: a purported invariant can become obsolete, be invalidated by a changed requirement, or disappear when the earlier Delete question dissolves the requirement. The absolute can preserve stale facts even when the document’s own method says to remove their need. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:71:35. [Line 69](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:69): “distinction-carrying names no machine consumes.” A distinction can be valuable to human readers even when no machine interprets the semantic distinction. Human-facing identifiers, headings, and labels are ordinary counterexamples, and readability is the second declared priority. The phrase neither defines “distinction-carrying” nor accounts for human consumers. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:73:36. [Line 73](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:73): “Reject theoretical problems.” The absolute heading conflicts with the narrower explanation that only cases with “no realistic path to occurring” are excluded. Threat modeling, race analysis, disaster recovery, and cryptographic reasoning are theoretical before occurrence but can have substantial practical value. The two readings lead to opposite treatment of rare, high-impact failures. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:75:37. [Line 74](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:74): “You may be given the project’s forward plan. A mechanism that will be needed at scale is not a valid deletion.” The file acknowledges that the roadmap may be absent but gives no rule for deciding whether a mechanism will be needed at scale in that reachable case. The reviewer cannot distinguish planned capacity from speculative machinery without the optional input. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:77:38. [Line 75](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:75): “A required field whose value nothing parses here may still be needed elsewhere.” “Here” and “elsewhere” have no defined boundaries. If “elsewhere” includes integrations or files outside the supplied documents, the reviewer can never establish that a field has no consumer; the consumer test becomes an unbounded negative search. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:79:39. [Line 76](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:76): “A change that reintroduces a recurring human step … is not a simplification.” Recurring human approval can be an intentional safety, consent, legal, or privilege boundary and can simplify the system by avoiding unreliable autonomous policy. The categorical statement is broader than the operator-cost principle can support. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:81:40. [Line 78](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:78): “The project’s rulings are recorded inline in the document and the documents it links.” This conflicts with line 3, which says the 18 dispositions are “in git history at this file,” and with its reference to a rejected draft available only in Git history. A reviewer following only the asserted inline locations can miss rulings the document itself places elsewhere. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:83:41. [Lines 84 and 92](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:84): “WHAT — the precise change” is required “For each finding,” but “‘the rest is already lean’ is a finding.” A leanness certification proposes no change, so it cannot satisfy the mandatory WHAT field and likewise has no meaningful migration COST or change CONSEQUENCES. The report schema and finding definition are incompatible. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:85:42. [Line 86](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:86): “which priority from the order above pays for it.” The metaphor has two incompatible readings: the sacrificed priority “pays” the loss, or the winning higher priority pays by justifying it. Because the priorities are ordinal objectives rather than budgets, the field does not tell the reviewer which side of the trade to name. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:87:43. [Line 87](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:87): “COST — migration effort against what is already built.” The assignment guarantees only MD files and their linked documents, not the current implementation, deployment state, or inventory of built components. When those are absent, migration effort cannot be determined from the permitted inputs. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:89:44. [Line 88](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:88): “every sentence elsewhere in the document, and every test, that becomes false or stale” and “the full blast radius.” The assignment does not guarantee tests or code as inputs, and with multiple MD files “the document” has no unique referent. “Every” and “full” also require exhaustive knowledge of hidden integrations and transitive effects without bounding the search, making literal completion impossible in ordinary reviews. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:91:45. [Line 92](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:92): “Say explicitly which areas are already minimal.” “Areas” has no defined decomposition or granularity, and proving that an area is minimal requires ruling out all alternative designs. The instruction therefore requires open-ended negative work without a clear stopping point. Confidence: sure.
./md-review-records/2026-08-11-sanity-checker-prompt-draft/codex-hunt-good.md:93:46. [Line 97](/home/nedlern/agents/choirmaster/docs/drafts/sanity-checker-prompt-draft.md:97): “a deliberate `none`, so an explicit answer is mechanically forced.” A mechanism can force the token `none`; it cannot mechanically establish that the choice was deliberate. A caller can copy a default, provide a placeholder, or select `none` without considering an issue. The example overstates the guarantee supplied by the forcing function. Confidence: sure.
