./md-review-records/2026-08-13-seat-first-prompt/claude-hunt-good.md:41:Conflicts with the definition in the checkout's `CLAUDE.md`, which this same file (line 19) designates as binding. `CLAUDE.md` line 8: "the git-gatekeeper (`scripts/git-gatekeeper.py check-in` …) is the permanent path — one program, one credential, one door; agents never push to main. Until its credential work lands (activation waits on build slice 6), the gate is dormant and the interim lane applies: commit to the working branch, push it, and the user's Mac-side agent reviews and merges." `seat-first-prompt.md` line 25: "Changes reach `main` only through the user's Mac-side review seat". The project document frames the Mac-side route as an explicitly *interim* lane with a named expiry condition and names the permanent path; this file states the interim lane as an unconditioned standing rule and never mentions the gatekeeper at all. Harm: on the day slice 6 lands and the gate activates, every seat's zero-context boot document still asserts the superseded route as the only one, and a seat reading a gatekeeper-era brief (`gatekeeper-instructions.md` is entirely about that program) will find its brief and its boot prompt describing different doors with no stated precedence. The shared clause "never push to `main`" survives either way, but the routing does not. Confidence: sure.
