./md-review-records/2026-08-13-seat-first-prompt/claude-hunt-floor.md:23:   This conflicts with the root `CLAUDE.md`'s definition of the same rule: *"How a change reaches main: the git-gatekeeper (...) is the **permanent path** — one program, one credential, one door; agents never push to main. **Until its credential work lands** (activation waits on build slice 6), the gate is dormant and **the interim lane applies**: commit to the working branch, push it, and the user's Mac-side agent reviews and merges."* CLAUDE.md frames the Mac-side-review path as a temporary, conditional stand-in for the git-gatekeeper (the stated permanent path); this file states it as an unconditional "standing rule" using the absolute word "only," with no mention of the gatekeeper or the condition under which the rule changes. Once the gatekeeper activates, this sentence becomes false as written, and an agent reading only this file (its declared zero-context starting point) would not know the gatekeeper exists as an alternative path at all. Category (h) — quoting both: this file's *"Changes reach `main` only through the user's Mac-side review seat"* vs. CLAUDE.md's *"the git-gatekeeper ... is the permanent path ... Until its credential work lands ... the interim lane applies."* Additionally, CLAUDE.md calls this reviewer *"the user's Mac-side agent,"* while this file calls it *"the user's Mac-side review seat"* — a second, smaller terminology mismatch in the same sentence. Confidence: sure on the conflict; sure on the terminology mismatch being present, unsure whether the author considers "seat" and "agent" interchangeable enough to not matter.
